it's a hundred percent clear that they  had no idea who was who was in that  vehicle and just lit it up hi I'm Tracy  Oppenheimer freezin TV today we're  talking with Christopher Driskill he's  the attorney for Maggie Carranza and her  mother Emma Hernandez these were the two  women who were shot on Thursday in  Torrance  when the police were on the manhunt for  Christopher Dorner they shot up their  truck because it resembled Christopher  Dorner struck Christopher thanks for  talking to us thanks for having me this  whole event is so unbelievable can you  clear up from the details from yesterday  what happened to Maggie and Emma they  were obviously in search of the suspect  they had a description of the suspect  they had a description of the suspects  vehicle the two women obviously do not  match that description in any way  different race different weight  different height different sex obviously  different clear the make of the car the  model of the car nothing matches and  they opened up on the car I've attempted  to count the number of bullet holes in  the rear of the car I think I've gotten  over 40 but also there were bullet holes  in other vehicles there were bullet  holes in homes nearby and so not only  were the lives of these two women  threatened but also of the individuals  in the neighborhood they definitely were  on edge and I think that the individuals  perhaps wanted to become the hero and  get the bad guy  and I guess that's why we have a phrase  jump the gun they jump the gun they they  open fire on the vehicle without  thinking without regard for police  protocols police procedures and they  it's a miracle that they didn't murder  these two innocent women but they came  pretty close and what what state are  Maggie and Emma in right now well  obviously they're traumatized they're in  a state of shock I don't think that  either one of them  has truly processed this event yet the  71 year old female who was in the ICU  has been medicated she has two bullet  wounds  she has a bullet wound to well to door  back one in the lower right-hand portion  of her back and one in her shoulder do  you think your clients will ever be  properly compensated for their injuries  and damages yeah I'd like to hope so  we'll see what happens  we've dealt with Los Angeles Police  Department before and first of all we  would hope that they do a honest and  thorough investigation of what occurred  and why it occurred and you know really  one of the the main points here is that  this really could have been anyone it  was someone in a truck that didn't match  the truck that they were looking for it  could have been you it could have been  me just someone traveling through the  neighborhood and we obviously need to  make sure that this does not repeat  itself because it's an absolute miracle  that these two women did not die they're  calling it my mistaken identity and by  labeling it as such and having the media  label it as such it isn't this an error  in itself I'm not sure that we can  really call it mistaken identity because  obviously they never made eye contact  they never visualized the two  individuals in the car if they had  obviously it would have been clear that  they did not have the right person is  there a certain mindset that overtakes  cops when they're when they're being  pursued I mean in this case Dorner is  being called the cop killer  I think perhaps they're sort of a an  us-versus-them philosophy that develops  sort of a sort of a war philosophy they  police officers frequently refer to each  other as brothers much like military  personnel and when an officer is injured  or goes down or is threatened the team  mentality I think really steps up and so  you just have that concern that  individual citizens rights are going to  get trampled when the officers are  attempting to get justice for their  brothers what greater message does this  kind of thing send about the LAPD Dorner  was saying I mean his whole manifestos  about police abuse and covering this  stuff up is this kind of ironic I think  there's sort of a triple layer of irony  here where we have an individual who is  complaining about the lack of justice  and use of excessive force within LAPD  and and in order to remedy that he uses  excessive force and murder someone with  no regard for their rights and then in  their attempt to take this person into  custody the LAPD in turn uses excessive  force and unfortunately these two women  are the innocent victims of that  Christopher I thank you so much for  taking the time to talk to us no problem  Friesen TV I'm Tracy Oppenheimer
 

